@node Pairs and lists
@section Pairs and lists
A @define{pair} (sometimes called a @define{dotted pair}) is a record
structure with two fields called the car and cdr fields (for historical
reasons).  Pairs are created by the procedure @code{cons}.  The car and
cdr fields are accessed by the procedures @code{car} and @code{cdr}.
The car and cdr fields are assigned by the procedures @nc{set-car!}  and
@nc{set-cdr!}.

@cindex list @subentry definition
Pairs are used primarily to represent lists.  A @define{list} can be
defined recursively as either the empty list or a pair whose cdr is a
list.  More precisely, the set of lists is defined as the smallest set
@var{X} such that

@itemize
@item
The empty list is in @var{X}.

@item
If @var{list} is in @var{X}, then any pair whose cdr field contains
@var{list} is also in @var{X}.
@end itemize

The objects in the car fields of successive pairs of a list are the
elements of the list.  For example, a two-element list is a pair whose
car is the first element and whose cdr is a pair whose car is the second
element and whose cdr is the empty list.  The length of a list is the
number of elements, which is the same as the number of pairs.

@cindex empty list
The empty list is a special object of its own type.  It is not a pair,
it has no elements, and its length is zero.

@quotation
@i{Note:} The above definitions imply that all lists have finite length
and are terminated by the empty list.
@end quotation

The most general notation (external representation) for Scheme pairs is
the @quotedbl{dotted} notation @ns{(@r{@varI{c}} . @r{@varII{c}})} where
@varI{c} where is the value of the car field and @varII{c} is the value
of the cdr field.  For example @nc{(4 . 5)} is a pair whose car is 4 and
whose cdr is 5.  Note that @ns{(4 . 5)} is the external representation
of a pair, not an expression that evaluates to a pair.

A more streamlined notation can be used for lists: the elements of the
list are simply enclosed in parentheses and separated by spaces.  The
empty list is written @samp{()}.  For example,

@lisp
(a b c d e)
@end lisp

@lisp
(a . (b . (c . (d . (e . ())))))
@end lisp

are equivalent notations for a list of symbols.

A chain of pairs not ending in the empty list is called an
@define{improper list}.  Note that an improper list is not a list.  The
list and dotted notations can be combined to represent improper lists:

@lisp
(a b c . d)
@end lisp

is equivalent to

@lisp
(a . (b . (c . d)))
@end lisp

Whether a given pair is a list depends upon what is stored in the cdr
field.  When the @nc{set-cdr!} procedure is used, an object can be a
list one moment and not the next:

@lisp
(define x (list 'a 'b 'c))
(define y x)
y              @ok{(a b c)}
(list? y)      @ok{#t}
(set-cdr! x 4) @unspecified{unspecified}
x              @ok{(a . 4)}
(eqv? x y)     @ok{#t}
y              @ok{(a . 4)}
(list? y)      @ok{#f}
(set-cdr! x x) @unspecified{unspecified}
(list? x)      @ok{#f}
@end lisp

Within literal expressions and representations of objects read by the
@code{read} procedure, the forms @ns{'@sv{datum}}, @ns{`@sv{datum}},
@ns{,@sv{datum}}, and @ns{,@atchar{}@sv{datum}}, denote two-element
lists whose first elements are the symbols @code{quote},
@nc{quasiquote}, @nc{unquote}, and @nc{unquote-splicing}, respectively.
The second element in each case is @angle{datum}.  This convention is
supported so that arbitrary Scheme programs can be represented as lists.
That is, according to Scheme's grammar, every @angle{expression} is also
a @angle{datum}.  Among other things, this permits the use of the
@code{read} procedure to parse Scheme programs.

@deffn procedure pair? obj

The @code{pair?} predicate returns @code{#t} if @var{obj} is a pair, and
otherwise returns @code{#f}.

@lisp
(pair? '(a . b)) @ok{#t}
(pair? '(a b c)) @ok{#t}
(pair? '())      @ok{#f}
(pair? '#(a b))  @ok{#f}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure cons @parI{obj} @parII{obj}

Returns a newly allocated pair whose car is @varI{obj} and whose cdr is
@varII{obj}.  The pair is guaranteed to be different (in the sense of
@code{eqv?}) from every existing object.

@lisp
(cons 'a '())        @ok{(a)}
(cons '(a) '(b c d)) @ok{((a) b c d)}
(cons "a" '(b c))    @ok{("a" b c)}
(cons 'a 3)          @ok{(a . 3)}
(cons '(a b) 'c)     @ok{((a b) . c)}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure car pair

Returns the contents of the car field of @var{pair}.  Note that it is an
error to take the car of the empty list.

@lisp
(car '(a b c))     @ok{a}
(car '((a) b c d)) @ok{(a)}
(car '(1 . 2))     @ok{1}
(car '())          @lispnote{error}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure cdr pair

Returns the contents of the cdr field of @var{pair}.  Note that it is an
error to take the cdr of the empty list.

@lisp
(cdr '((a) b c d)) @ok{(b c d)}
(cdr '(1 . 2))     @ok{2}
(cdr '())          @lispnote{error}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure set-car! pair obj

Stores @var{obj} in the car field of @var{pair}.

@lisp
(define (f) (list 'not-a-constant-list))
(define (g) '(constant-list))
(set-car! (f) 3) @unspecified{unspecified}
(set-car! (g) 3) @lispnote{error}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure set-cdr! pair obj

Stores @var{obj} in the cdr field of @var{pair}.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure caar pair
@deffnx procedure cadr pair
@deffnx procedure cdar pair
@deffnx procedure cddr pair

These procedures are compositions of @code{car} and @code{cdr} as
follows:

@lisp
(define (caar x) (car (car x)))
(define (cadr x) (car (cdr x)))
(define (cdar x) (cdr (car x)))
(define (cddr x) (cdr (cdr x)))
@end lisp
@end deffn

@c This formatting isn't great.
@ifhtml
@deffn {cxr library procedure} caaar @var{pair}
@deffnx {cxr library procedure} caadr @var{pair}
@deffnx {cxr library procedure} cdddar @var{pair}
@deffnx {cxr library procedure} cddddr @var{pair}

These twenty-four procedures are further compositions of @code{car} and
@code{cdr} on the same principles.  For example, @code{caddr} could be
defined by

@example grammar
(define caddr (lambda (x) (car (cdr (cdr x)))))@r{.}
@end example

Arbitrary compositions up to four deep are provided.
@end deffn
@end ifhtml
@ifnothtml
@deffn scheme/cxr caaar pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr caadr pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr cadar pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr caddr pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr cdaar pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr cdadr pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr cddar pair
@deffnx scheme/cxr cdddr pair
